# Routh I.Art.394-398 — the hodograph of a Kepler orbit is a CIRCLE
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Arts. 394-398. The hodograph is the curve traced by the tip of the
# velocity vector drawn from a fixed origin (Art. 394, after Hamilton): draw
# OQ parallel to the motion with |OQ| proportional to the speed, and the locus
# of Q is the hodograph. Its arc-rate ds'/dt is the acceleration itself.
#
# THE THEOREM. For a central force Routh finds it more convenient to draw OQ
# PERPENDICULAR to the tangent, because v = h/OY with OY the perpendicular
# from the centre onto the tangent (Art. 395) — which makes the hodograph the
# POLAR RECIPROCAL of the orbit. For the inverse square the reciprocal of an
# ellipse about a focus is a circle, so:
#
#     the hodograph of every Kepler orbit -- ellipse, parabola or hyperbola --
#     is a circle, or an arc of one.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. The orbit is an ellipse traversed at wildly varying
# speed: at perihelion the particle is fast and close, at aphelion slow and
# far. Yet the velocity vector, replotted from a common origin, moves on a
# perfect circle at constant... nothing. The speed is not constant and the
# angular rate is not constant; only the LOCUS is circular.
#
# THE ALGEBRA. With the apse along x and h the angular momentum per unit mass,
#
#     v = (mu/h) * ( -sin(theta),  e + cos(theta) )
#
# so  v - (0, mu*e/h)  has constant length mu/h. The hodograph is therefore a
# circle of radius mu/h centred at (0, mu*e/h) -- offset from the origin by
# exactly e times the radius. e < 1 puts the origin inside the circle (closed
# orbit), e = 1 on it, e > 1 outside.
#
# THE NUMBERS. mu = 1, e = 0.6, a = 1, so the perihelion distance is
# a(1-e) = 0.4 and the perihelion speed is sqrt(mu(1+e)/(a(1-e))) = 2. Then
#
#     h        = 0.4 * 2 = 0.8
#     radius   = mu/h     = 1.25
#     centre   = (0, mu*e/h) = (0, 0.75)
#
# The cells below sample norm(velocity - [0, 0.75, 0]) at four points spread
# around one orbit. Every sample must read 1.25.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: an e = 0.6 ellipse — fast sweep through perihelion on the
# +x side, slow crawl at aphelion. The invariant being demonstrated is in the
# printed numbers: whatever the speed, the velocity tip never leaves its circle.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_hodograph_circle
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_hodograph_circle.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_hodograph_circle.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }

# Baselines: speed at perihelion is 2, the hodograph radius is mu/h = 1.25,
# and the centre sits at (0, 0.75).
norm(obj1.velocity)
norm(obj1.velocity - [0, 0.75, 0])

# Four samples spread around one orbit (period 2*pi). Every one reads 1.25
# even though the speed itself ranges from 2 down to 0.5.
run pi / 2 steps 2
norm(obj1.velocity)
norm(obj1.velocity - [0, 0.75, 0])
run pi / 2 steps 2
norm(obj1.velocity)
norm(obj1.velocity - [0, 0.75, 0])
run pi steps 2
norm(obj1.velocity)
norm(obj1.velocity - [0, 0.75, 0])
reset

set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [0.4, 0, 0], velocity = [0, 2, 0] }
energy
laplace 1

scene create
scene zoom in
scene set_time_step 0.01
